=== 2026-07-14T18:21:35.964809+00:00 envs/cedar-poc/bin/cedar authorize --help ===
Evaluate an authorization request

Usage: cedar authorize [OPTIONS] --entities <FILE>

Options:
  -l, --principal <PRINCIPAL>
          Principal for the request, e.g., User::"alice"

  -a, --action <ACTION>
          Action for the request, e.g., Action::"view"

  -r, --resource <RESOURCE>
          Resource for the request, e.g., File::"myfile.txt"

  -c, --context <FILE>
          File containing a JSON object representing the context for the request. Should be a (possibly empty) map from keys to values

      --request-json <FILE>
          File containing a JSON object representing the entire request. Must have fields "principal", "action", "resource", and "context", where "context" is a (possibly empty) map from keys to values. This option replaces --principal, --action, etc

      --request-validation <REQUEST_VALIDATION>
          Whether to enable request validation. This has no effect if a schema is not provided
          
          [default: true]
          [possible values: true, false]

  -p, --policies <FILE>
          File containing the static Cedar policies and/or templates. If not provided, read policies from stdin

      --policy-format <POLICY_FORMAT>
          Format of policies in the `--policies` file

          Possible values:
          - cedar: The standard Cedar policy format, documented at <https://docs.cedarpolicy.com/policies/syntax-policy.html>
          - json:  Cedar's JSON policy format, documented at <https://docs.cedarpolicy.com/policies/json-format.html>
          
          [default: cedar]

  -k, --template-linked <FILE>
          File containing template-linked policies

  -s, --schema <FILE>
          File containing the schema

      --schema-format <SCHEMA_FORMAT>
          Schema format

          Possible values:
          - cedar: the Cedar format
          - json:  JSON format
          
          [default: cedar]

      --entities <FILE>
          File containing JSON representation of the Cedar entity hierarchy

  -v, --verbose
          More verbose output. (For instance, indicate which policies applied to the request, if any.)

  -t, --timing
          Time authorization and report timing information

  -f, --error-format <ERR_FMT>
          The output format to use for error reporting

          Possible values:
          - human: Human-readable error messages with terminal graphics and inline code snippets
          - plain: Plain-text error messages without fancy graphics or colors, suitable for screen readers
          - json:  Machine-readable JSON output
          
          [env: CEDAR_ERROR_FORMAT=]
          [default: human]

  -h, --help
          Print help (see a summary with '-h')

